{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 6,
  "simulation_id": "sim-03",
  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B030",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B028",
    "B009"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed: I now propose B030 alone rather than the previous two-part IceCube and lattice-clock configuration. Optical lattice clocks are a mature, independently reproduced experimental advance with a sharply identifiable physical architecture: magic-wavelength confinement enables many atoms to improve clock performance without the corresponding perturbation of the clock transition. Katori's originating concept and Ye's decisive experimental development form a compact and complementary credit line. The demonstrated 10^-18-class precision has durably changed the scope of frequency comparison and fundamental measurement, whether or not a later application supplies a single headline discovery.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation remains that B030 rewards an enabling measurement capability, and its most dramatic possible results concerning gravitation or fundamental constants have not yet been identified here as completed discoveries. The historical boundary between the lattice-clock invention and the wider optical-frequency-metrology enterprise must also be documented carefully. I have withdrawn IceCube from the preferred configuration because the present record leaves too much uncertainty about whether Halzen alone can fairly represent the conception, construction, calibration, and analysis of a large collaboration.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that independently demonstrated 10^-18-class lattice-clock performance and the compact Katori-Ye attribution make B030 ready for recognition even without a particular subsequent discovery.",
        "response": "I agree. As an experimentalist, I regard independent reproducibility at this level as especially weighty evidence that the physical architecture, rather than an isolated technical record, has opened a durable new measurement regime. The citation should remain confined to the invention and development of optical lattice clocks."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson favors WMAP as a completed observational transformation and argues that IceCube's large collaboration makes single-person attribution less secure.",
        "response": "The WMAP case properly reminds us to distinguish a completed empirical transformation from a promising capability. It also reinforces my decision to remove B028 from my preferred configuration: unresolved documentary attribution is not a minor defect when one person would receive credit for a collaboration. For B030, by contrast, the two-person conceptual and experimental line is sufficiently clear on the supplied record, while the instrument itself is the achievement being recognized."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson favors circuit QED as a mature controllable quantum-optical architecture, while noting its broad theoretical and experimental lineage.",
        "response": "I accept that circuit QED is a substantial completed platform discovery. Its attribution boundary nevertheless remains less settled than the Katori-Ye line, especially under a three-person limit. The lattice-clock case therefore provides the more defensible final public configuration without relying on prospective applications."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argues that observable geometric and topological phase effects have exceptional conceptual maturity, but acknowledges the difficulty of joining the historically distinct Aharonov-Bohm and Berry discoveries.",
        "response": "The conceptual importance of B042 is clear, but its proposed unified prize still requires a stronger common historical boundary than the current record provides. B030 is more coherent as a single achievement and has a more direct attribution line."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether documentation can sharply delimit Katori's original lattice-clock concept and Ye's decisive experimental role from the wider development of optical-frequency metrology.",
      "Whether the committee should await a landmark fundamental-physics result enabled by optical lattice clocks, despite their already demonstrated and independently reproduced measurement capability.",
      "Whether any sole-laureate formulation for IceCube can adequately meet the attribution standard for a large collaboration."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile. I work in astroparticle physics and participate in large detector collaborations, which heightens my attention to the attribution problem for IceCube, but no specific collaboration or personal connection to the proposed B030 laureates is supplied."
  }
}
